---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Proof - DataTech Consulting and Contracting"
  description="How DataTech works, shown through systems we own and operate: a Columbus rental portfolio, Baked Well, a certified organic food business, and DataTech's own intake. No client data, no invented results."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">Proof, from work we own</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We do not publish client work without written permission, so this page
      shows systems we built for businesses we own and operate. Each piece is
      written the same way: the situation, what was built, and what changed.
      Outcomes are described without client data, and we do not publish
      numbers we cannot stand behind.
    </p>

    <section>
      <div class="proof-piece">
        <h2>1. Columbus rental portfolio</h2>
        <h3>Situation</h3>
        <p>
          A short-term and mid-term rental portfolio in Columbus, operated as
          Columbus Urban Retreats. Guest questions arrive at all hours across
          booking platforms. Pricing needs regular review as dates fill or
          sit open. The property owner needs a clear monthly statement of
          what happened.
        </p>
        <h3>What was built</h3>
        <p>
          Automation around the work the operators were doing by hand: guest
          messaging that answers common questions and routes the rest to a
          person, pricing support that reviews open dates and flags gaps,
          cleaning and check-in coordination, and owner reporting that pulls
          the month together into one packet.
        </p>
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>
          Guest messages get a fast first response at any hour, with a person
          stepping in where the answer is not approved in advance. Pricing is
          reviewed on a rhythm instead of when someone remembers. Owner
          reporting is assembled from the operating systems rather than
          rebuilt by hand each month. This is the same pattern we sell to
          service businesses: answer fast, capture the details, hand off with
          a summary.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="proof-piece">
        <h2>2. Baked Well, a certified organic food business</h2>
        <h3>Situation</h3>
        <p>
          Baked Well is a certified organic bakery business we co-own. It
          sells through its own website and other channels, works with
          ingredient suppliers, and has to keep product, pricing, and
          certification records straight across all of them.
        </p>
        <h3>What was built</h3>
        <p>
          Systems for the parts of the business that repeat: online ordering
          and product presentation on the website, price and listing upkeep
          across sales channels, ingredient order review, and organised
          records for the organic certification the business holds.
        </p>
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>
          Product and price changes are made once and checked across
          channels, instead of edited channel by channel from memory.
          Ordering and certification paperwork lives in a known place and can
          be found when a supplier, a certifier, or a customer asks. The work
          is a food business, not a tech demo, which keeps the systems
          honest: they have to hold up on a baking day.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="proof-piece">
        <h2>3. DataTech's own intake</h2>
        <h3>Situation</h3>
        <p>
          Our own enquiries used to depend on a free-form message. A buyer had
          to guess what to tell us, and we had to ask follow-up questions
          before we could say whether a fixed-scope build fit.
        </p>
        <h3>What was built</h3>
        <p>
          This website is the working example of our Lead Intake offer. The
          contact form asks for the company, the offer of interest, and where
          work is being missed, then turns the answers into a structured email
          summary, the same shape we deliver to a client's team. The offers
          themselves are stated with scope and price, so an enquiry starts
          from a shared understanding instead of a blank page.
        </p>
        <h3>What changed</h3>
        <p>
          An enquiry now arrives with the context a fit call needs. We can
          read it, judge the fit, and reply with a next step instead of a
          list of basic questions. If you want to see it work, send the form
          on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="note">
        Note on this page: these are our own businesses, described in our own
        words, with no client data and no results we cannot verify. Client
        case studies will be added only when a client agrees in writing to
        the exact form published.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:24px;"><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Book a 20-minute fit call</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
